A||||9||Aussieken||rear brake pads||||6
Z||000000||Aussieken||10-26-1999||07:43 PM||||I was trying to change the rear pads on my '88 Formula and according to my Haynes manual you have to rotate the piston to get it to go back into the caliper. I started on the right (facing forward) and rotated the piston clockwise like the manual says with a homemade tool to engage the slots in the piston. After rotating with difficulty for a while I realised that the piston was going nowhere. Thinking I might have it wrong I tried counter clockwise , but but this only made the piston come out further. What am I doing wrong ? Can changing a set of pads be this hard ? Very few Fieros in Australia so there's no help here. ||||reg||6
Z||000001||lowCG||10-26-1999||10:02 PM||||I think you have to rotate the screw from the back side of the caliper.<BR>They never used E-brakes in "Road Warrior",or did they?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||10-26-1999||10:44 PM||||I just did mine recently.  It's easy as heck once you figure out how to do it.  Here goes:<BR>Unattach the parking brake cable, the metal nub is sitting right there, just slip that out of it's slot.  Next, take a screwdriver and pop off the PBrake return spring.  Now, get a wrench on that large nut, it is in the middle of the caliper.  It will be hard to break loose, you will have to keep the parking brake lever from moving with a large screwdriver while you try to break this damn thing loose.  Now, take off the parking brake lever, the small rubber gasket, and the VERY tiny, clear plastic seal underneath the rubber washer.  What you now see is the actuator screw.  You are now free to use a c clamp or water pump pliers to compress the piston, but make sure that the tool does not come in contact with the actuator nut!  Now put your pads on, remount the caliper, and reverse the procedure.  The real *****  here is the return spring.  It is fairly stiff.  The method that seems to work best is to get the spring compressed enough so that it can rest on the lower part of the return lever (do this by hand) Next, take a long, skinny screwdriver, and put it into the spring long ways.  This will be used to push the spring UP.  Now, take a big old fat screwdriver, and use that one to push the spring towards the trunk, while you use the long one to eek it up to the point where it locks in.  It's a real pain in the butt, but I belive there is a special tool for this, I don't know.  It worked for me though!  Happy motoring!||||reg||2
Z||000003||fierospeeder||10-27-1999||12:07 AM||||Try this, if you havn't, go to your resevoir and open the top, so it wouldn't be under pressure, and the piston in the caliper should go back in while turning it clockwise, unless you have to rebuild the caliper if its really stuck.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||10-27-1999||12:26 AM||||Unless the parking brake is not adjusted properly (the levers are not on the stops) the rear brakes should not be under any pressure. If they were, wouldn't they just pop out of the bores when you removed the caliper?||||reg||1
Z||000005||rcn491||10-27-1999||01:13 AM||||The 88 rear calipers are different from earlier models. I had the same problem. The piston would screw out, but not in. The problem is that the parking brake screw is connected to the piston thru a clutch device.<BR>If the piston is slightly cocked in the bore it will slip on this clutch instead of retracting. Try removing the rubber seal, then rotating the piston.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||10-27-1999||01:33 AM||||Replacing the rear pads was the first thing I did to a Fiero,guess I got lucky,didn't have to take anything apart,just pressed,and turned the piston at the same time,was an '86 though.<BR>Some cars will push a little fluid out of the resevoir,and onto your spare tire/painted compartment,when the pistons are returned if they were filled up before the pads went south.<BR>I'd keep close watch on the rear brakes after replacing the pads and not cleaning the gunk out of the inside of the caliper and off the piston;just check to see if one wheel is getting hotter than the other,after driving a ways to see if the pistons are sticking in the calipers.<BR>Oh,the Holdens with discs in the rear will have a similar setup with the screw/lever deal.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 10-27-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Boxdin||10-27-1999||11:44 PM||||On 2 88 formulas so far I've had to rebuild  the rear calipers because the piston would not bottom out enough to get the pads in. on one car, there  did seem to be a ratchet type mechanisn inside the piston, and on the other one, none. Whats the deal there?||||reg||5
Z||000008||Aussieken||10-31-1999||06:32 PM||||Thanks Shiner, you had the answer. The Haynes manual is a real bum steer. There's no need to take the caliper off at all!. Just take the handbrake lever off the back of the caliper and the piston pushes right in, no problem. This is a trap for young players.||||reg||7
Z||000009||Shiner||11-01-1999||12:33 AM||||Aussieken,<P> Glad to be of some assistance.  You are right; the hayne's manual throws you off.  It is a good manual for some things, but it is HORRIBLE for others.  I have to get an '87 factory service manual for my GT.  That's where the good stuff is at.||||reg||7
